ed by this script** and has no artefact backing it in the snapshot.\n  \n  **C/I is never actually exercised.** `c_over_i_db` is the literal string `\"infinite (isolated co-channel beam fixture)\"` (L107). It is a constant label, not a computed quantity — no interference term appears anywhere in the C/N expression (L89–96). So the ledger verifies a **C/N-only, single-beam** budget; the co-channel interference path of the physics model is untested by this ledger. Say so plainly: the \"C/I\" column is decorative.\n  \n  **Second gap:** `q10 = 0.7749486236990524` (boresight) and `0.5204588857996268` (edge) are **hardcoded magic numbers** (L150, L157). They appear nowhere else in the repo (`grep` over all `.py`/`.md`: only this script and the ledger MD). They are not derived from `SHADOW_SIGMA_DB` / `SCINTILLATION_LOSS_DB` / `RICIAN_K_FACTOR_DB`. Every row of the \"Load, mode, and headroom ledger\" that depends on q10 (predicted SINR, transmitted mode, all `NO_MODE`/clears verdicts) is therefore **not independent** — it is a value copied from the engine being audited. The C/N, EIRP, G/T, bandwidth and capacity headlines *are* independent; the mode-selection verdicts are not.\n  \n  **(iii) Independent recomputation** (`PYTHONPATH=src python3`, constants read from `src/mcrl/physics_v025/constants_v025.py`; no deps needed, uv not required):\n  \n  | quantity | my value | claimed |\n  |---|---:|---:|\n  | T_sys | 242.294454 K | 242.294454 K ✓ |\n  | k | −228.599167173 dBW/K/Hz | −228.599168 ✓ |\n  | G/T | 11.156565 dB/K | 11.156565 ✓ |\n  | B | 166.666667 MHz | ✓ |\n  | edge elevation | 25.801425° | 25.801425° ✓ |\n  | C/N boresight | **19.196747413 dB** | 19.196747 (report §4) ✓ |\n  | C/N edge | **9.851770 dB** | 9.851770 ✓ |\n  | edge penalty | 9.344978 (=6.020600 FSPL + 3.000000 point + 0.324378 gas) | ✓ |\n  | max SE | 3.710855833 bit/s/Hz | ✓ |\n  | beam capacity | **618.475972 Mbit/s** | 618.475972 ✓ |\n  | n=12 (600 Mbit/s) | eligible, 32APSK 8/9 | ✓ |\n  | n=13 (650 Mbit/s) | **no eligible mode** (req SE 3.900 &gt; 3.710856) | ✓ |\n  \n  All six load rows reproduce exactly (0.014244 / 0.022836 / 0.061891 / 0.122497 / 0.196393 / 0.532262 W; headrooms 20.638560 / 18.588560 / 14.258560 / 11.293582 / 9.243582 / 4.913582 dB; NO_MODE / QPSK 1/4 / QPSK 3/5 / NO_MODE / NO_MODE / QPSK 1/2). QPSK 1/4 threshold −1.441812460 matches frozen `SINR_MIN_DB` exactly.\n  \n  **Two rounding discrepancies found:**\n  - Ledger MD table (line 20) prints C/N boresight **19.196748**; true value is 19.196747413 → correct round is 19.196747. Report §4 has it right; the MD table is wrong in the last digit.\n  - Ledger MD line 10 states noise power \"−122.539 dBW\"; correct is **−122.537245 dBW** (the 5.575393e−13 W value is right). ~1.8 mdB error in a stated dB figure.\n  \n  **(iv) Reconciliation with engine flags — NO.** `main()` (L135–165) emits a JSON blob and nothing else: it never loads, parses or compares against any engine receipt, and emits no agreement/disagreement verdict. The \"Simulator reconciliation\" table in the MD *narrates* engine flags (`decode flag`, `nominal power-feasible`, `50-Mbit/s target attained`) side by side with the ledger's own columns, but there is no executable comparison, no assertion, no pass/fail field, and no immutable artefact for it. Verdict: **numbers stated side by side, not reconciled.** The paragraph \"It returned the same setpoint powers to displayed precision\" is an unverifiable prose claim in this snapshot.\n  \n  ## JOB B — QUANTILE SWEEP\n  \n  **SHA-256 CONFIRMED.** `sha256sum /home/sat/mcrl-v025-codex-ws-engine/.tmp/stage4h/quantile-sweep-corrected.json` = `288f87a42df57e111d4f6bee7a94c04e6b9292a123e95106c72e9ab2d9cf2565`, matching both the prompt and the sidecar `.sha256`. File mode `-r--r--r--`.\n  \n  **Report-only / not selecting alpha — CONFIRMED.** JSON top level: `status = \"SMOKE_NOT_MATRIX\"`, `primary_alpha_remains_0.10 = true`, and `variants.alpha_0.10_PRIMARY.primary = true` with all other variants `primary: false`. Both MD (line 3) and report §5 line 51 state alpha=.10 is primary by preregistration and the sweep does not select it. Consistent.\n  \n  **Quarantined-world only — CONFIRMED.** `world_domain = \"V025_SMOKE/world/1\"`, `world_sha256 = 8a8187ff…`. Single world, `executed_steps = 2` (two anchors).\n  \n  **Excluded from any merge — WEAKLY SUPPORTED.** The `SMOKE_NOT_MATRIX` status plus the quarantined `V025_SMOKE` domain are the mechanism, and both documents label it TRAIN-only diagnostic. But there is **no explicit `merge: false` / exclusion field in the JSON**, and no merge-exclusion sentence in `SEAL-PACKAGE-DRAFT-2026-09-08.md` (grep for \"merge\" returns nothing there). Exclusion rests on convention, not on a machine-checkable flag.\n  \n  **Arms/agreement counts — RECOMPUTED, ALL MATCH.** Counting `same_trajectory_as_primary` over the 14 arms in each `comparisons_to_primary` block, and independently re-deriving from raw arm `bits`/`joules`/`availability` (exact equality) — both methods agree:\n  \n  | view | recomputed | report §5 | differing arm | delta (bit/J) |\n  |---|---:|---:|---|---:|\n  | alpha=.05 | **13/14** | 13/14 ✓ | E1_U1 | +38,019.128 ✓ |\n  | alpha=.10 primary | **14/14** | 14/14 ✓ | none | 0 |\n  | alpha=.25 | **14/14** | 14/14 ✓ | none | 0 |\n  | nominal | **13/14** | 13/14 ✓ | NOMINAL_GREEDY | −10,027,588.356 ✓ |\n  \n  Arm count is 14 in every variant. E1_U1 at alpha=.05: 73.353542 Gbit / 11,991.996 J / 6.116875 Mbit/J / avail 0.472926 vs primary 72.907238 / 11,993.579 / 6.078856 / 0.474043 — matches MD line 22 exactly. NOMINAL_GREEDY nominal 44.754389 Gbit / 5,568.429 J / 8.037166 Mbit/J / 0.294734 vs primary 81.916796 / 4,534.620 / 18.064755 / 0.511011 — matches MD line 18 exactly.\n  \n  **48 boundaries and closed-loop — CONFIRMED in JSON.** `endpoint_boundaries_per_anchor = 48`, `selection_stage2_m = 48`, `selection_boundary_indices = [0]` (the sealed one-boundary fallback), `selection_workers = 4`, `each_arm_keeps_own_trajectory = true`, `paired_common_exogenous_world = true`, `deadline_and_fallback_accounted = true`. Timing `provider_seconds = 22.082`, `sweep_seconds = 412.088` match MD line 7.\n  \n  **One prose error:** MD line 20 says \"FULL recorded two deadline fallbacks; certified S_UNI reached the same endpoint totals with no deadline miss.\" JSON: `FULL.deadline_misses = 2`, `S_UNI.deadline_misses = 0` — correct. Verified across all four variants.\n  \n  ## JOB C — GATE TABLE + MEAN/P95\n  \n  **Evidence file located:** `/home/sat/mcrl-v025-codex-ws-engine/.tmp/stage4h/gate-three-decisions.json` (mode `-r--r--r--`; sidecar sha `2ae58f69…` matches `sha256sum`). This is the *same* file the report calls \"the earlier immutable evidence file\" — there is only one.\n  \n  **(1) MEAN and P95 present and recomputed.** Raw `decision_times_s = [9.920571741997264, 8.879806935001398, 5.949833396996837]`.\n  - mean = **8.250070691331834** → report's 8.250071 ✓\n  - max = **9.920571741997264** → 9.920572 ✓\n  - p95: sorted [5.9498, 8.8798, 9.9206], h = (n−1)·0.95 = 1.90, x[1] + 0.90·(x[2] − x[1]) = 8.879806935 + 0.90·1.040764807 = **9.816495261297678** → 9.816495 ✓\n  \n  **Convention: linear interpolation on (n−1)p** — numpy's default `method=\"linear\"` / Hyndman–Fan type 7 / Excel `PERCENTILE.INC`. Nearest-rank (type 1) would give 9.920572 instead, identical to the max. Anchor totals reproduce the same way: mean 20.658407696988434, p95 = 20.5406748559908 + 0.9·(21.3154492029862 − 20.5406748559908) = 21.237971768286663 ✓.\n  \n  **Can 3 samples support a p95? No.** With n=3 the type-7 p95 is a fixed affine blend of only the top two order statistics (0.1·x₍₂₎ + 0.9·x₍₃₎). It carries no information beyond max and second-max, has no tail resolution above the 66.7th empirical percentile, and its value is entirely an artefact of the interpolation rule (type-1 vs type-7 differ by 0.104 s here — larger than the 0.079 s margin to the 10 s gate). The honest statement is \"max 9.920572 s over 3 anchors\"; the p95 adds no evidence and should be read as a formatting requirement satisfied, not a tail estimate. The gate itself passes on the max, which is the binding quantity — but with only **0.79% headroom** on a time-sliced single-CPU host.\n  \n  **Decision-time definition is internally consistent:** per-anchor `fading_quantile_cache + catalogue + stage1_scores + stage2_forecasts + selection + validation` reproduces `decision_times_s` to ~0.0025 s (9.918062 vs 9.920572, etc.). The excluded phases are `c2_forecast_validity_diagnostic` (≈4.4–4.5 s) and `s_uni_comparator` (≈6.1–9.3 s).\n  \n  **(2) The \"FAIL\" file — verbatim.** In `gate-three-decisions.json` the field is literally:\n  \n  ```\n  \"status\":\"FAIL\"\n  ```\n  \n  alongside `\"unit_s\":62.1153701049916` and `\"surface\":\"one-boundary/M48\"`. (Note the same file already carries `decision_max_s = 9.9206` and `anchor_total_max = 21.3154`, both inside their gates — so the FAIL was computed against `unit_s`, i.e. the 3-anchor aggregate, 62.115 s vs an apparent 60 s bound. Cross-check: Σ anchor totals = 61.975223 s, plus 0.140 s harness overhead = 62.115 s.)\n  \n  **Is re-labelling legitimate? Yes — the JSON's status field, not the report, is the defective artefact.** The declared gates are:\n  - Contract v1 **§F2**: \"the coordinator's compute budget is **10 s wall on the declared worker count**\".\n  - `V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-4D-GATE-2026-09-09.md` item 5: \"The wall-time measurement … uses **the full path catalogue → stage 1 → stage 2 → selection → validation**.\" — exactly the phase set the report's `decision` column sums.\n  - `V025-CONTROLLER-DECISIONS-4C-GATE-2026-09-08.md` item 4: \"**per anchor total** … **≤ 60 s**.\"\n  \n  There is **no declared gate on a three-anchor aggregate**. 62.115 s vs 60 s compares a unit-level sum against a per-anchor bound — a category error in the emitting script. The report's reading (per-decision &lt;10 s, per-anchor &lt;60 s) is the sealed